<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gotthard Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St. Gotthard Pass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gotthard Pass is a 2,106-metre pass in the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> linking Uri in the north with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> in the south. It became a major trade route around 1230, after a bridge was built across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen</a> gorge, giving the valley communities of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> strategic value. Railway (1882), road (1980) and base (2016) tunnels later made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s chief north–south Alpine crossing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass.jpg" alt="Gotthard Pass" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Figugegl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gotthardpass_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The opening of a route over the <b>Gotthard Pass</b> in the early thirteenth century, made possible by bridges through the Schöllenen gorge, gave the valleys of Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden new strategic and commercial value, and Uri in 1231 and Schwyz in 1240 obtained charters placing them directly…</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lucerne grew around the monastery of Saint Leodegar where the Reuss leaves Lake Lucerne, and boomed after the opening of the <b>Gotthard</b> route in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Canton of Lucerne</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Gotthard Pass</b> was opened to traffic in the early 13th century, the valley gained strategic value, and in 1231 King Henry VII bought it back from the Habsburgs and granted it imperial immediacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-uri">Canton of Uri</a></li></ul></section>
